+/* What a context needs to be built again after a snapshot.
+ *
+ * Every command the guest sends goes through vkr_snap_dispatch, which
+ * notes what the command looked up, created and destroyed.  Commands that
+ * create or destroy objects, bind memory, update descriptors or record
+ * command buffers are kept, as the guest encoded them.  Ids are the
+ * guest's own, so replaying the kept commands in a fresh context gives the
+ * same objects under the same ids.  Commands nothing alive needs any more
+ * are dropped now and then (vkr_snap_gc).
+ *
+ * A snapshot (vkr_snap_save) stops every thread at a command boundary,
+ * waits for the GPU, and writes the kept commands, the contents of device
+ * memory and images (read back through a staging buffer), the shared
+ * memory the context made (rings, reply streams), fence, event and
+ * semaphore states, and where each ring stands.  vkr_snap_restore does it
+ * all again in a new context; vkr_snap_resume starts its rings.
+ */
+
